12:33:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's one of the most intuitive objections, and I completely get why it looks that way. A kid bouncing off the walls, tapping their desk, squirming in their chair—it screams "too much energy." But the neuroscience flips that intuition on its head: that hyperactivity isn't an excess of energy; it's a desperate attempt to *generate* the arousal the brain can't create on its own. ### Fidgeting Is Self-Stimulation, Not Energy Release The child's body isn't burning off surplus fuel. It's acting as a manual generator for an under-aroused brain. Movement stimulates the release of dopamine and norepinephrine. Squirming, tapping, and bouncing are unconscious, compensatory behaviors that try to wake up the sleepy prefrontal cortex. Think of it like this: you're driving a car with a dying battery. The engine keeps stalling. To keep it running, you have to constantly rev the engine, even when you're sitting still at a red light. To an observer, it looks like you're a reckless driver with a too-powerful engine, eager to race. In reality, you're just trying to keep the thing from dying. That revving is the fidgeting. It's not excess power; it's a survival mechanism for a system that can't maintain idle. ### Why Stimulants Quiet the Body If the hyperactivity were true excess energy, giving a stimulant would be like adding nitro to an already over-revving engine. The fidgeting would explode into chaos. Instead, stimulant medication often dramatically reduces the squirming and tapping. Why? Because the medication supplies the dopamine and norepinephrine the brain was starving for. The generator (the body) can finally stop working overtime. The car's battery is charged, so the driver no longer has to rev the engine just to keep it alive. ### The Calm Is the Proof Again This is the same paradoxical calming effect we've been circling around. The hyperactive body isn't a sign of too much neural stimulation; it's a sign that the brain's arousal networks are so underpowered that the motor system has to constantly crank the engine to keep the lights on. When coffee or medication provides the missing fuel, the frantic self-stimulation ceases, and the child can sit still—not sedated, but finally sufficiently awake to not need their body to keep them alert.
