ng to fix a neurochemical deficit over a lifetime. The psychiatrist knows the filter is broken and under-aroused. The medication targets that directly. But telling a sobbing seven-year-old who just threw a chair that his under-aroused prefrontal cortex failed to inhibit his amygdala is worse than useless. So the world splits in two: the everyday language of fast action, and the medical language of deep mechanism. ### The Ordinary Meaning of "Overstimulated" Is Just More Useful There's one more layer. The word "overstimulated" in common English just means "I got more input than I could handle." It doesn't carry a neurochemical claim about why. It's a description of outcome, not cause. When an ADHD kid melts down at a birthday party, the outcome is indeed that they got more sensory input than their brain could manage. That's true regardless of whether the root cause was a broken filter or oversensitive sensors. The word works because it's silent on the "why" and loud on the "what now." The filter model isn't hidden because it's fringe. It's hidden because it's not useful for the people who need to act fast. They don't need to know why the dam broke; they just need to move the village downstream.
