ually a clinical clue about which subtype someone has. ### The Spillover Problem, Amplified Even within the classic under-aroused group, stimulant response is dose-dependent and brain-chemistry-dependent. We talked about spillover: the drug doesn't just hit the prefrontal cortex. It hits the whole brain and body. For some, the therapeutic window is vanishingly small. The dose needed to nudge the prefrontal cortex into function also triggers intolerable anxiety in the amygdala or tachycardia in the body. They're not "overstimulated at baseline" in the way you're arguing—their filter is still broken—but the medication's side effects are so severe they can't tolerate the fix. That's a treatment limitation, not a proof that the underlying problem was global overarousal. ### Misdiagnosis and Comorbidity Some people diagnosed with ADHD who get worse on stimulants may have been misdiagnosed entirely. Their inattention and hyperactivity could stem from primary anxiety, PTSD, bipolar disorder, or sensory processing issues without the classic ADHD dopamine deficit. If you give a stimulant to someone whose core problem is a hyperactive fear circuit, you're going to see a disaster. That doesn't disprove the filter model for true ADHD; it highlights how imprecise our diagnostic labels are. ### The Under-Arousal Model Survives Because of the Responders, Not the Non-Responders The filter model isn't a claim that every single person with attention problems is under-aroused. It's a model that explains why a specific, large subgroup of people with ADHD experience the paradoxical calming effect. The non-responders don't invalidate the model; they reveal its boundary. They might be a different subtype, a different diagnosis, or simply too sensitive to the spillover. The model remains the best explanation for the phenomenon you originally asked about: why coffee and stimulants can bring calm, not chaos, to so many people who look terribly overstimulated.
