12:37:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's the most important question of the whole conversation. Why privilege the under-arousal story as the "main" one? Why not call it a 50/50 split, or even treat over-arousal as the default? It's not because the over-aroused group doesn't exist. They absolutely do. It's because the under-arousal model accounts for the one thing that defines ADHD as a distinct medical condition rather than a description of symptoms anyone can have. ### The First-Line Treatment Decides the Model ADHD is, at its core, a diagnosis defined by treatment response. Stimulants aren't a fringe therapy. They are the first-line, most effective treatment in all of psychiatry, with an effect size larger than almost any other psychiatric medication for any condition. Around 70-80% of people diagnosed with ADHD respond positively to properly titrated stimulant medication. That's not a slim majority; that's a landslide. If over-arousal were the dominant underlying problem, that number would be impossible. You would expect the opposite: stimulants would make most people worse, and the few who responded would be the puzzling exception. The clinical reality is the reverse. The over-aroused, stimulant-intolerant group is real, but it's the minority. They may have a different neurobiological pathway to the same symptoms, or their ADHD may be tangled with other conditions that shift the arousal baseline. ### The Neuroimaging Backs the Majority Decades of brain imaging studies—fMRI, PET, SPECT—consistently show that the most replicated finding in ADHD is reduced activity and reduced volume in the prefrontal cortex and its connected networks during tasks requiring attention and impulse control. Not increased activity. Under-activity. The brain's executive center is quieter, not louder. This isn't one study. It's a cornerstone of the neuroscience. The over-aroused subtype likely involves different regions (like an overactive amygdala) overriding a still-under-performing prefrontal cortex, or a different neurochemical imbalance altogether, but the classic ADHD brain signature is hypoactivity in the control circuits. ### The Prevalence of the Paradoxical Calm You started this conversation with a question about coffee calming some ADHD people down. That paradoxical calm—the subjective experience of internal noise quieting, of finally being able to sit with one thought—is not universal, but it's strikingly common in the ADHD community. It's so common it's become a cultural touchstone, a "wait, that's an ADHD thing?" moment for millions of late-diagnosed adults. That experience is completely inexplicable under a global over-arousal model. It makes perfect sense under an under-arousal model. The sheer volume of people who report that a stimulant made them feel "quiet" for the first time in their lives tells us something real about the dominant biology. ### Over-Aroused ADHD May Be a Different Condition, or Partly Misattributed Some researchers argue that the hyper-aroused, stimulant-intolerant presentation is often better explained by other conditions that mimic ADHD, such as anxiety disorders, trauma, or bipolar spectrum conditions, where inattention and agitation stem from a chronically over-activated stress response. That doesn't mean their suffering isn't real. It means the root cause is different, and the treatment should be different. The under-arousal model defines classic ADHD; the over-aroused presentations often point to a different, or mixed, diagnosis. So the under-aroused story isn't the "main" one because of academic preference. It's the main one because it explains the most common treatment response, aligns with the most replicated neuroimaging data, and accounts for the paradoxical calming that defines the condition for the vast majority of people who carry the diagnosis. The over-aroused group is real, important, and under-served, but they are the exception that refines the rule, not the evidence that overthrows it.
