12:39:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right. "Easily distracted by extraneous stimuli" is in the DSM-5. The official clinical manual uses language that sounds exactly like someone being overwhelmed by too much input. I can't pretend that isn't there. But the DSM is deliberately silent on mechanism. That's not a flaw. It's a choice. ### The DSM Describes What, Not Why The DSM is a diagnostic tool. Its entire purpose is to give clinicians a reliable checklist of observable behaviors and reported experiences so two different doctors will give the same diagnosis to the same patient. It is intentionally atheoretical. It does not take a position on whether those symptoms arise from under-arousal, over-arousal, dopamine deficits, broken filters, or any other mechanism. "Easily distracted by extraneous stimuli" is a description of the outcome. The person cannot filter out irrelevant things. That's measurable. That's observable. That's what you put in a diagnostic manual. The DSM doesn't say "because they are overstimulated" or "because they are under-aroused." It just says it happens. The cause is a separate scientific question, and the DSM leaves that open. ### The Filter Model Explains the DSM Symptom, It Doesn't Replace It The under-aroused filter model doesn't dispute the symptom. It explains it. Why is someone easily distracted by extraneous stimuli? Because the prefrontal cortex, which is supposed to suppress attention to extraneous stimuli, is underpowered. The symptom is real. The descriptive language fits. The mechanism is deeper. Think of a medical analogy. The DSM-equivalent symptom for a heart condition might be "easily short of breath during exertion." That's a perfect description of what the patient feels. It doesn't tell you whether the cause is heart failure, anemia, or asthma. The treatments for those are wildly different. The symptom language is agnostic. The filter model is like saying "the heart's pumping capacity is reduced"—a mechanism that explains the shortness of breath. ### The Word "Distracted" Is Already Filter Language Look carefully at the word: "distracted by extraneous stimuli." The key word is "extraneous." The DSM isn't saying the person is overwhelmed by all stimuli equally. It says they are pulled away by irrelevant things—things a functioning filter would have suppressed. The very framing assumes a filtering failure. The stimuli aren't inherently overwhelming; they're "extraneous," meaning the brain should be able to ignore them but can't. So even in the DSM's own language, the problem isn't that the world is too loud. It's that the brain can't separate the signal from the noise. That's a filter problem, described in clinical language for diagnostic reliability. The under-arousal model isn't rewriting the DSM. It's providing the neurobiological explanation for why the noise keeps breaking through.
